WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.041
Welcome to AeroTwin: an interactive digital-twin prototype for monitoring a synthetic aero piston engine.

00:00:07.041 --> 00:00:15.087
This short walkthrough explains the two concept clips, then shows what the working prototype actually does.

00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:19.667
The first supplied clip depicts an aircraft emergency scenario.

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.738
The second shows a flight-monitoring and safe-landing concept.

00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:26.283
Both are illustrative visuals only.

00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:37.478
They are not recordings of AeroTwin, real UAV footage from this application, or evidence that crash detection or emergency dispatch is connected.

00:00:37.478 --> 00:00:48.811
In the prototype, a simulator generates changing engine telemetry: RPM, temperatures, oil and fuel pressures, fuel flow, vibration, and operating conditions.

00:00:48.811 --> 00:00:59.064
The digital twin updates from that stream, and the dashboard visualizes the same selected engine state as it changes.

00:00:59.064 --> 00:01:09.603
For early warning, an Isolation Forest looks for unusual sensor patterns, while a Random Forest classifies simulated fault types.

00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:15.149
The interface shows the predicted class, confidence, and contributing signals.

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:21.805
Both models were trained on generated synthetic data, not validated real-engine recordings.

00:01:21.805 --> 00:01:26.432
Component health and the remaining-life indicator are simulated estimates.

00:01:26.432 --> 00:01:36.200
Mission reliability combines twin health and simulated mission phases to make the impact of a fault easier to understand.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.341
These numbers are demonstrations, not certified airworthiness or service-life decisions.

00:01:41.341 --> 00:01:46.136
The fleet view lets you select among independent virtual engines.

00:01:46.136 --> 00:01:51.891
The virtual UAV page visualizes a route linked to the selected engine.

00:01:51.891 --> 00:01:55.728
You can pause, resume, and inject demonstration faults.

00:01:55.728 --> 00:02:01.962
These controls affect software simulation only; they do not pilot a real aircraft.

00:02:01.962 --> 00:02:06.913
The assistant receives the latest twin snapshot with each question.

00:02:06.913 --> 00:02:16.320
On the public static site, it uses browser-side evidence routing; a backend can optionally call a configured language model.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:23.251
The Data Lab checks uploaded CSV signals for configured limit breaches and persistent trends.

00:02:23.251 --> 00:02:26.717
Prototype thresholds are not validated operating limits.

00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:32.692
The dashboard SOS drill only previews a simulated incident brief.

00:02:32.692 --> 00:02:40.460
It does not detect a real crash, locate responders, or send an alert.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:55.997
In summary, AeroTwin demonstrates a connected monitoring workflow: telemetry, twin, anomaly evidence, fault classification, health, mission context, and operator-facing explanations, all inside a safe synthetic prototype.

